import{_ as n,P as i,d as o,e as a,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const v=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.11 Open-Ended Undertaking Action (HV11)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.11 Open-Ended Undertaking Action (HV11)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1926,"xkLessons":{"A.11":{"title":"A.11 Open-Ended Undertaking Action (HV11)","lead":"Retain actual failure and unknown outcomes, and closely examine refusal and exit. Effectiveness is not voluntariness, and sacrifice does not prove love. The ending checks the non-authorization boundaries of all eleven variables together.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4650–4759, original-site location /theory/appendix-a/a-11#section-a-11. The current full text is in the theory-v2 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","Short quotation: “It does not diagnose whether someone has love.” Open-ended undertaking action observes only evidenced actions, real costs, direction, voluntariness, alternative explanations, and consequences. It is restricted to the human-domain clauses “Open-Ended Undertaking” and “Action Uptake and Classification of Responsibility” and is constrained by the normative premise “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted”; it does not score love by the amount of sacrifice or treat refusal as moral failure. The name must not be used to requisition the actor’s actions in reverse. [T-main]","Route one (record of action and cost) may still record candidate undertakings, cost distributions, coercion risks, gaps in stopping rights, and protective needs when action costs are grounded but voluntariness, refusal, or exit are insufficiently supported. What pauses is route two’s naming of “open-ended undertaking,” not the retention of known costs. The requirements of the detailed description cannot force actors into refusal or disclosure they cannot safely perform. [T-main]","Route two (bounded voluntary undertaking) requires real action costs and a clear direction, with separate support for voluntariness, genuine refusal, and conditions for exit or negotiation, and checking relevant alternatives such as fear, dependency, interests, and performance. Outcomes are separately recorded as achieved, not achieved, harmful, or unknown. Genuine failed attempts are not erased because they did not succeed, nor does grounded voluntariness disappear because outcomes are unknown. [T-main]","Route three (structural consequences) additionally requires an eligible channel-effect test that identifies an above-threshold effect through the specified channel on preselected outcomes, and also requires the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals.” Coerced action can also be effective, so route three cannot backfill the voluntariness of route two; bounded voluntariness is not automatically effective either. The three routes are not a bad-to-good ladder, still less do effects prove love, goodness, justification, or unlimited responsibility. [T-main]","Ethical naming remains constrained by the normative premises set out in the governance clause “Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate” (§16.14); reaching route three cannot replace them. An action description is also not an individual or group obligation: one person voluntarily contributing once does not mean similar members all consent to ongoing burdens. Representative relations, cost spillovers, genuine exit, and proxy protection must be added separately when moving to a larger scale. Human voluntariness and meaning diagnoses do not apply to nonhuman systems. [T-main；T-gov]","Observed costs include time, resources, opportunities, and physical and psychological costs, but this variable makes no personality or clinical diagnoses. Separate immediate contributions, ongoing depletion, and recovery lags; beneficiaries, dependents, and alternative uptake providers may all be affected. Retain positions unable to refuse or exit safely or obscured by moral pressure; silence cannot fill in voluntariness. [T-main]","The appendix-wide notes at the end of this lesson must also be read: among the nine scale axes, network topology scope is written N, whereas the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion are the five normative premises (N1–N5); the two differ. Identity belongs to the identity criterion; relational channels belong to inputs, uptake carriers, or the conditions for causal claims; membership positions belong to affected positions, the local exclusion zone, and the items on observation. None can impersonate a scale axis. Expanding aggregation level, spatial extent, organizational level, or scope of influence does not expand the scope of authorization. [T-main]","The common table specifies outputs for each of the eleven variables: Structural Domain, candidate domains, boundaries, and supplementary-evidence needs; Boundaries and Interfaces, boundaries, interfaces, exclusions, and testing needs; Orientation Anchor, anchors, heterogeneous expression, and comparisons; Generative Node, candidates for generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, and transitions; Action Uptake Layer, uptake carriers and responsible parties, costs, capacity, stopping rights, and needs for additional resources and redistribution; Drive–Uptake Chain, connectivity, lags, losses, interruptions, costs, and uptake; Feedback Write-Back, acceptance, changes in institutional elements, execution, persistence, write-back, and procedural repair; Conditional Potential Field, candidate conditions, heterogeneity, masking, and risk reduction; Structural Load, loads, capacity, recovery, local overload, and load-reduction needs; Evolutionary Phase, components, prototypes, mixtures, bounded mechanisms or historical increments, the independent “Orderly Exit and Benign Demise,” and uncertainty under the selected route; Open-Ended Undertaking Action, voluntariness, costs, direction, alternatives, consequences, and needs for stopping and protection. [T-main]","All are descriptions or requirements, not authorization for actual testing, load reduction, additional resources, repair, protection, responsibility attribution, advancement, or exit. Any real adjustment separately requires normative bridging, explicitly stated normative premises, valid itemized authorization, and operational procedures. Even obvious protective needs require checking independently existing authority; this detailed description cannot be an authorization document. Authorized restoration revokes undertaking labels and requirements, actually restores refusal, exit, records, and resource states, and verifies completion. [T-main]","NASA ASRS’s confidentiality page describes voluntary reporting, deidentification, and restricted incentives; the first item on its separate Caveats page explicitly excludes accident or criminal-activity reports from confidentiality and penalty-immunity protections. This is used to examine the distinction between an institution’s description of itself as voluntary and its concrete protective conditions. It does not infer that every reporter can genuinely refuse, characterize them as loving, or claim support under routes two and three of this framework. [P-ASRS；P-ASRS-C]","Item-by-item study (“Source text” below retains the item’s complete content; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Code. The source text gives the code only in parentheses at the end of the heading and uses the name in the text. Interpretation: the code is a short identifier within the code system of Appendix B; it tracks the same variable and does not indicate that a real-world object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified code. The source text does not list this item separately. Interpretation: the qualified form serves only to avoid collisions between identical names; references to this variable must still carry object and evidential status. [T-main]","03 | Name. Source heading: Open-Ended Undertaking Action. Interpretation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for particular people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Proposition role. The source text does not list this item separately; under §2.2 it belongs to the role of “human-domain clauses.” Interpretation: this marks it as a proposition of the human domain; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Role in the system. The source text lists it as one of the eleven human variables of Part 7 (§7.3). Interpretation: this is the definition of a human variable, specifying how it enters analysis; listing the items does not establish that any empirical test holds. [T-main]","06 | Proposition. Source text: Open-ended undertaking examines only actual costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, and structural consequences. It does not diagnose whether someone has love. Interpretation: read this proposition together with the subsequent support routes and ceilings; do not extract an unconditional causal or normative conclusion from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope of application. Source text: Undertaking in human relationships, organizations, institutions, and public action. Interpretation: scope lists task domains that may be studied, not empirical support for every object within them. [T-main]","08 | Conditions for suspension. Source text: Suspend naming the action “open-ended undertaking” under route two if voluntariness or the ability to refuse or exit cannot be confirmed safely. Candidate actions, costs, coercion risks, gaps in the right to stop, and protection needs may still be recorded under route one. Diagnosing personality or love is always prohibited. Interpretation: inability to confirm voluntariness safely pauses route two’s naming, while route one retains costs, coercion risks, and protective needs; diagnosing love is always prohibited. [T-main]","09 | Inferential premises. Source text: None. Interpretation: Inferential premises provide the upstream content needed to establish a proposition. Upstream candidate or supported status must be carried through unchanged; citation does not automatically upgrade it. [T-main]","10 | Method requirements. Source text: The normative premise “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted” (§14.3); conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7). Interpretation: method requirements constrain evidence gathering and expression; meeting method requirements does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Domain restriction. Source text: The human-domain clause “Open-Ended Undertaking” (§7.1); the human-domain clause “Action Uptake and Classification of Responsibility” (§7.1). Interpretation: writing “none” means only that no such domain restriction is listed, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a listed restriction also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Objects of application. Source text: None. Interpretation: objects of application cannot replace the current object’s definition; writing “none” is an explicit statement, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Support routes. Source text: Route one, record of action and cost. Conclusion level: Normative boundary. Conditions: There are grounds for the action and its related costs. Direction, who benefits, and the consequences can each be recorded as known or unknown, but voluntariness or the ability to refuse or exit has not yet received sufficient support. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Describe candidate undertakings, the distribution of costs, coercion risks, gaps in the right to stop, and protection needs. Ceiling: The action must not be called open-ended undertaking. Love or personality must not be diagnosed, and continued undertaking must not be required. Route two, bounded voluntary undertaking. Conclusion level: Normative boundary. Conditions: There are grounds for the action and its actual costs, its direction is clear, voluntariness and genuine conditions for refusal, exit, or negotiation receive separate support, and relevant alternative explanations have been checked. The outcome is recorded as “realized,” “unrealized,” “harmful,” or “unknown,” and structural success is not a condition for entry. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record a description of bounded voluntary undertaking and the outcome status. Failure or an unknown outcome does not cancel supported facts about the action and its voluntariness. Ceiling: Description of the action only. Do not raise individual undertaking to a group duty or authorize conscription, protection, or resource arrangements. Route three, structural consequences. Conclusion level: Conditional effect. Conditions: An eligible channel-effect test (§4.4) shows that the action has an above-threshold effect on the preselected structural outcome through the specified channel. Further inferential premises: This channel-effect test. Further method requirements: Conditions for causal claims (§2.4); Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Determine the action’s structural consequences within the specified channel, object, and time window. Ceiling: A structural effect does not establish voluntariness, love, goodness, normative justification, unlimited responsibility, or authorization to act. Support for an effect under route three cannot fill an evidence gap in route two. Coerced action can also have identifiable effects. Interpretation: gather evidence separately for the candidate costs of route one, the bounded voluntariness of route two, and the structural effects of route three; effective coercion cannot supply voluntariness, and genuine failure is not absence of action. [T-main]","14 | Can infer. Source text: A description of bounded undertaking actions and their consequences. Interpretation: these are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached and with what strength remain limited by the selected route and materials. [T-main]","15 | Cannot infer. Source text: A diagnosis of whether someone has love; that sacrifice amounts to love; that responsibility amounts to unlimited undertaking; that refusal amounts to moral failure. Interpretation: these are common boundary violations, not avoided by rewording; inspect actual inference and downstream use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale. Source text: Aggregation level covers individual acts of undertaking, cases involving actors, populations of relationships or organizations, distributions of costs and voluntariness, and aggregation rules. Spatial extent covers sites of relationships and care, organizational boundaries, digital public spaces, and the reach of benefits across domains. Time span covers windows for immediate action, sustained undertaking, exhaustion, recovery, and effects across generations. Organizational level covers roles in action, relationships or teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. Causal level covers events of undertaking, mechanisms of interaction between actions and costs, meso-level relational structures, institutional arrangements of responsibility, and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers original actions and costs, sequences of undertaking, cases involving actors, distributions of costs and voluntariness, consequence metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct actors and beneficiaries, dependents, those indirectly taking over, second-order spillovers, and impacts across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers dependencies in care, links of benefit, alternative arrangements for taking over, exit paths, and cost networks across domains. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for demands to take up work, resource use, refusal, stopping, protection, and remedy separately. Differences in voluntariness, costs, and exit need to be preserved. Interpretation: state all nine axes individually; particularly, scope of influence creates no authority on the axis of jurisdiction and authorization, and lower observation resolution requires retention of compression loss. [T-main]","17 | Effective object. Source text: Human arrangements for undertaking in which action, cost, direction, voluntariness, conditions for refusal, and consequences need to be examined separately. Routes one, two, and three are judged under their own conditions, without first screening actions by their structural consequences. Interpretation: this describes the variable’s corresponding object; choosing a lower-threshold descriptive route cannot treat functional meaning in the name as established causation. [T-main]","18 | Kept across scales. Source text: Costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, consequences, and rights to stop. Interpretation: what is kept across scales is a comparative responsibility retained during transfer, not a declaration that variable values remain constant at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Must be added when moving to a larger scale. Source text: Distribution of voluntariness; relations of representation; cost spillovers; a genuine means of exit and protection provided through representatives. Interpretation: moving to a larger scale is not copying and enlarging the original table. Where materials on new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, restrict conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Changes with scale. Source text: Forms of undertaking, positions bearing costs, and beneficiaries can change. Interpretation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; reconstruct correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Objects to which it does not apply. Source text: Nonhuman systems without intentionality, voluntariness, responsibility, or the capability for meaning. Interpretation: the inapplicability restriction prevents unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experiential concepts; it does not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Cannot be raised to. Source text: Individual undertaking raised to a group duty; the concept of human undertaking transferred into the nonhuman core. Interpretation: inference from local to whole requires separate proof; broad impact, a grand name, or formal similarity cannot supply representativeness and authorization. [T-main]","23 | States. Source text: Candidate; voluntary and bounded; risk of coercion; unilateral exhaustion; stopping or exit. Interpretation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require time windows, grounds, and records of changes. [T-main]","24 | Observables. Source text: Time and resources invested, opportunities forgone, and bodily and psychological costs of the action; whether refusal, exit, stopping, and renegotiation are genuinely available; direction of the action, positions receiving benefits, and detectable structural consequences; alternative explanations such as coercion, fear, dependence, interests, and acting for show. Interpretation: observable indications tell us where to seek materials; a single indication’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements. Source text: Actions and costs; conditions for refusal and exit; alternative explanations; benefits and consequences. Interpretation: obtain listed evidence according to route and task relevance; distinguish unknown, not yet collected, not applicable, and withheld for protection, without forcing disclosure to fill a table. [T-main]","26 | Inputs. Source text: Orientation anchor (§7.3.3); action uptake layer (§7.3.5); conditional potential field (§7.3.8); power and safety. Interpretation: this states what arrives from upstream and its eligibility; gaps cannot quietly disappear while passing between variables. [T-main]","27 | Outputs. Source text: Distribution of costs; states of relationships and institutions; stopping and repair. Interpretation: outputs identify downstream judgments that may depend on this variable; passing references guarantees neither downstream conclusions, actual repair, nor authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag. Source text: State immediate costs, sustained undertaking, exhaustion, and recovery lags. Interpretation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may differ in time; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extensions beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty. Source text: Record dependence, fear, hidden coercion, and safety of expression. Interpretation: direct uncertainty toward specific materials and positions; an overall confidence score cannot conceal fundamentally uncovered positions. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone. Source text: Positions where people cannot refuse safely, cannot exit, or are masked by moral pressure. Interpretation: exclusion zones limit current inferential coverage, not delete people or facts from the world; unavailability for safety is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions. Source text: Actors; beneficiaries; dependents; people taking over. Interpretation: record directly and indirectly affected parties together; visible members do not represent every cost bearer. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carriers. Source text: Specific actors; relational practices; arrangements for care or responsibility. Interpretation: a carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something; its name, completed construction, and arrival of the required function are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible parties. Source text: Those making demands; parties granting authorization; parties responsible as beneficiaries; parties responsible for remedy. Interpretation: identify who proposes, operates, or uses judgments; recording responsibility is not automatically a legal-liability adjudication or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status. Source text: Subject to the normative premise “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted.” It cannot be commanded or conscripted. Interpretation: the normative premise “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted” (§14.3) prohibits commanding or requisitioning this ethical designation; independently grounded responsibilities cannot be expanded without limit by this label. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling. Source text: Apply the limits for routes one, two, and three separately. Route one describes only costs, direction, risks, and candidate actions. With corresponding evidence, route two describes the action’s voluntariness and conditions for refusal and exit. Route three judges structural consequences within a limited scope only if the corresponding channel-effect test and causal conditions hold. Judgments at any level must not diagnose personality, prove love or goodness, infer a duty to undertake, or authorize real-world action. Ethical naming is separately subject to the requirements on normative premises in the governance clause “Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate” (§16.14). Reaching route three cannot take the place of that requirement. Interpretation: no route proves love, personality, goodness, or real authorization; ethical naming additionally requires the normative premises set out in the governance clause “Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate” (§16.14). [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling. Source text: This variable produces only descriptions of voluntariness, actual costs, direction, alternative explanations, structural consequences, and needs for stopping and protection. It does not authorize protective measures, demands to take up work, requisition of resources, or adjudication of personality. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures. Interpretation: the variable itself creates no real-world authority to impose dispositions; actual actions separately require checking choice, normative bridging, explicitly stated normative premises, valid itemized authorization, operational procedures, protections, and periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples. Source text: One-sided sacrifice that cannot be refused is praised as love or responsibility; a claim of undertaking lacks evidence of the stated action or cost. Actual failed attempts and cases with unknown outcomes are recorded separately. Their action descriptions must not be categorically withdrawn for lack of structural success. Interpretation: counterexamples restrict unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or gathering evidence anew, without automatically negating every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal. Source text: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), actors can refuse to be represented and explain coercion, costs, alternative explanations, and restrictions on exit through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original judgment about the undertaking or the original chain of demands. Interpretation: appeals must be safely accessible, free from retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not mean authority to correct errors exists. [T-main]","39 | Restoration. Source text: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline revoke the undertaking designation and related demands, and actually restore the state of refusal, exit, records, and resources. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified. Interpretation: actually cancel erroneous effects within valid authorization, correct downstream uses, and verify completion; changing document wording does not restore rights and resources to the affected person. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Check action costs and direction; where voluntariness cannot be safely checked, stop the naming under route two while retaining the risk record of route one.","Separately check voluntariness, genuine refusal, exit or negotiation, and alternative explanations.","Record outcomes as achieved/not achieved/harmful/unknown; failure does not delete grounded actions.","Structural consequences under route three separately require a channel-effect test, the conditions for causal claims, and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals”; effects cannot fill gaps in route two.","Add representation, distributions, spillovers, and protections across scales; distinguish network topology scope (N) from the five normative premises (N1–N5).","The eleven variables’ outputs generate requirements only; actual actions separately require checks of normative bridging, normative premises, itemized authorization, and operational procedures and can be independently corrected."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Bounded voluntariness","leftText":"Actions and conditions for refusal and exit are grounded.","rightTitle":"Structural success","rightText":"Whether the preselected outcome was achieved requires separate evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Effective action","leftText":"Identifiable effects may exist.","rightTitle":"Voluntary action","rightText":"Coercion can also be effective; the inference cannot run backward."},{"leftTitle":"A genuine failed attempt","leftText":"Action costs are grounded but the outcome was not achieved.","rightTitle":"An unsupported claim","rightText":"The claimed action or costs lack support; these are different cases."},{"leftTitle":"Network topology scope (N)","leftText":"Topological scope.","rightTitle":"Normative premises (N1–N5)","rightText":"The five normative premises (N1–N5); they cannot be entered interchangeably."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the source text: one-sided sacrifice that cannot be refused is praised as love or responsibility.","Counterexample in the source text: a claim of undertaking lacks evidence of the stated action or cost; actual failed attempts and cases with unknown outcomes, however, are recorded separately.","Teaching counterexample: one member voluntarily takes a shift, and everyone is then required to take shifts forever.","Boundary in the original: effects under route three cannot replace the voluntariness of route two or the premises for ethical naming in the governance clause “Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate” (§16.14)."],"questions":["Can real undertaking be recorded when structural outcomes are unknown?","Does greater sacrifice mean greater love?","Can effectiveness establish voluntariness?","Does a safety need automatically authorize me to provide protection?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"NASA ASRS explanations of voluntary reporting and institutional protection","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"NASA ASRS explanations of voluntary reporting and institutional protection","text":"The ASRS confidentiality page separately explains voluntary submission, confidential de-identification, and restricted incentives.","sourceLabel":"NASA ASRS: Confidentiality and Incentives to Report","sourceUrl":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html","sourceLocator":"Body text on voluntary submission, confidential de-identification, and restricted incentives, webpage lines 30–32","boundary":"Only institutional arrangements and data limitations; it does not represent every reporter’s conditions of freedom or diagnose personality or love.","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.11","start_line":4707,"end_line":4816,"startLine":4707,"endLine":4816}],"outputId":"section-a-11","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: failure does not erase action","text":"A constructed volunteer spends two hours preparing an event under arrangements allowing safe refusal and exit; weather cancels the event, so the intended activity does not occur. The two hours of action and supported voluntary conditions may still be described, with nonachievement recorded separately; success cannot be invented to call it “undertaking,” nor can love be diagnosed.","assumptions":"All events are fictional; safe refusal and exit are stipulated solely for teaching.","boundary":"Actual use of route two requires independent materials; a voluntary declaration alone does not pass it."},{"title":"Teaching construction: effective coercion still cannot become voluntary","text":"A constructed worker works overtime under threat of losing basic resources, and service output does improve. Even if an eligible channel-effect test later supports that effect, it does not supply the voluntariness, refusal, or exit that route two requires; retain the coercion risk and needs for stopping and protection.","assumptions":"The threat and outcome are fictional, separating effects from voluntariness without certifying real causation.","boundary":"Effects do not justify requiring continued undertaking; actual protection still requires independently valid authority and procedures."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: one-sided sacrifice that cannot be refused is praised as love or responsibility.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: a claim of undertaking lacks evidence of the stated action or cost; actual failed attempts and cases with unknown outcomes, however, are recorded separately.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: one member voluntarily takes a shift, and everyone is then required to take shifts forever.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: effects under route three cannot replace the voluntariness of route two or the premises for ethical naming in the governance clause “Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate” (§16.14).","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can real undertaking be recorded when structural outcomes are unknown?","answer":"When the other conditions of route two are supported, the fact of voluntary action may be retained while the outcome is recorded as unknown.","basis":"Success in outcomes is not an entry requirement."},{"question":"Does greater sacrifice mean greater love?","answer":"Such a diagnosis must not be made; costs, voluntariness, coercion, and consequences are examined separately.","basis":"The boundary set by the normative premise “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted.”"},{"question":"Can effectiveness establish voluntariness?","answer":"No. Coerced action may also be effective; route three does not supply route two.","basis":"Separation of the three routes."},{"question":"Does a safety need automatically authorize me to provide protection?","answer":"The variable only identifies needs; real measures require checks of normative bridging, normative premises, itemized authorization, operational procedures, and existing independent authority.","basis":"The shared action boundary of the eleven variables."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-routes.png","alt":"Route one: supported cost candidates; route two: bounded voluntary action; route three: specified structural effects. They cannot compensate for one another.","caption":"Based on the three routes in A.11."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-results.png","alt":"Achieved, unachieved, harmful, and unknown outcomes are separate from voluntariness; failure does not erase supported action.","caption":"Based on routes two and three of A.11; the example is fictional."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-common.png","alt":"The eleven variables generate only descriptions, gaps, and needs; keep network topology scope (N) apart from the five normative premises (N1–N5), and separately check normative bridging, normative premises, itemized authorization, and operational procedures for real action.","caption":"Based on the appendix-wide shared explanation after A.11."}],"diagramBrief":"The three figures respectively show the three routes, two-dimensional separation of outcomes and voluntariness, and the shared non-authorizing boundary of the eleven variables.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No voluntariness, personality, or moral diagnosis was made of any real actor.","ASRS institutional explanations cannot establish safe exit in every case, and the first Caveats item explicitly states confidentiality exceptions."],"taskId":"S-A-11","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.11, Chinese source lines 4228–4317","claim_support":["All 39 HV11 fields and three routes, the concluding nine-axis explanation, and the output table for the eleven variables."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-gov","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§16.14, Chinese source lines 3458–3471","claim_support":["GOV-14 normative boundaries for the ethical naming of open-ended undertaking."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-ASRS","title":"NASA ASRS: Confidentiality and Incentives to Report","url":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Webpage version at access","locator":"Body text on voluntary submission, confidential de-identification, and restricted incentives, webpage lines 30–32","claim_support":["ASRS explains confidential de-identification arrangements and restricted incentives for voluntary reports."],"limits":["The webpage body was read; institutional explanations on the website do not establish that every reporter can actually refuse, and do not constitute unlimited immunity or this framework’s R1/R2 certification."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-ASRS-C","title":"NASA ASRS: Caveats","url":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/caveats.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Webpage version at access","locator":"First body-text item and explanation of sanction limitations, webpage lines 29–31","claim_support":["Confidentiality and immunity from sanctions do not cover reports of accidents or criminal activities; on receipt, these are forwarded with names to the appropriate agencies. Immunity from sanctions has further limitations."],"limits":["The specified body text was read; this topic does not give current legal advice or describe limited institutional protection as absolute confidentiality."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"A.11　开放性承担行动（HV11）; Chinese source lines 4650–4759; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/source/S-A-11.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"c063deac831600f392448c56db932ed91ff3f80664aa6db24754256fc0326f62","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-11.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-11.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-11.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return o(),a("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Open-Ended Undertaking Action (HV11) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.11 Open-Ended Undertaking Action (HV11) {#section-a-11}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Retain actual failure and unknown outcomes, and closely examine refusal and exit. Effectiveness is not voluntariness, and sacrifice does not prove love. The ending checks the non-authorization boundaries of all eleven variables together.</span></div><ol><li>Proposition and Scope of Application</li></ol><p>Proposition: Open-ended undertaking examines only actual costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, and structural consequences. It does not diagnose whether someone has love.</p><p>Scope of application: Undertaking in human relationships, organizations, institutions, and public action.</p><p>Conditions for suspension: Suspend naming the action “open-ended undertaking” under route two if voluntariness or the ability to refuse or exit cannot be confirmed safely. Candidate actions, costs, coercion risks, gaps in the right to stop, and protection needs may still be recorded under route one. Diagnosing personality or love is always prohibited.</p><ol start="2"><li>Grounds and Limits on Conclusions</li></ol><p>Inferential premises: None.</p><p>Method requirements: The normative premise “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted” (§14.3); conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7).</p><p>Domain restriction: The human-domain clause “Open-Ended Undertaking” (§7.1); the human-domain clause “Action Uptake and Classification of Responsibility” (§7.1).</p><p>Objects of application: None.</p><p>Support routes:</p><p>Route one, record of action and cost. Conclusion level: Normative boundary. Conditions: There are grounds for the action and its related costs. Direction, who benefits, and the consequences can each be recorded as known or unknown, but voluntariness or the ability to refuse or exit has not yet received sufficient support. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Describe candidate undertakings, the distribution of costs, coercion risks, gaps in the right to stop, and protection needs. Ceiling: The action must not be called open-ended undertaking. Love or personality must not be diagnosed, and continued undertaking must not be required.</p><p>Route two, bounded voluntary undertaking. Conclusion level: Normative boundary. Conditions: There are grounds for the action and its actual costs, its direction is clear, voluntariness and genuine conditions for refusal, exit, or negotiation receive separate support, and relevant alternative explanations have been checked. The outcome is recorded as “realized,” “unrealized,” “harmful,” or “unknown,” and structural success is not a condition for entry. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record a description of bounded voluntary undertaking and the outcome status. Failure or an unknown outcome does not cancel supported facts about the action and its voluntariness. Ceiling: Description of the action only. Do not raise individual undertaking to a group duty or authorize conscription, protection, or resource arrangements.</p><p>Route three, structural consequences. Conclusion level: Conditional effect. Conditions: An eligible channel-effect test (§4.4) shows that the action has an above-threshold effect on the preselected structural outcome through the specified channel. Further inferential premises: This channel-effect test. Further method requirements: Conditions for causal claims (§2.4); Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Determine the action’s structural consequences within the specified channel, object, and time window. Ceiling: A structural effect does not establish voluntariness, love, goodness, normative justification, unlimited responsibility, or authorization to act. Support for an effect under route three cannot fill an evidence gap in route two. Coerced action can also have identifiable effects.</p><p>Can infer: A description of bounded undertaking actions and their consequences.</p><p>Cannot infer: A diagnosis of whether someone has love; that sacrifice amounts to love; that responsibility amounts to unlimited undertaking; that refusal amounts to moral failure.</p><ol start="3"><li>Scale and Object</li></ol><p>Nine-axis scale: Aggregation level covers individual acts of undertaking, cases involving actors, populations of relationships or organizations, distributions of costs and voluntariness, and aggregation rules. Spatial extent covers sites of relationships and care, organizational boundaries, digital public spaces, and the reach of benefits across domains. Time span covers windows for immediate action, sustained undertaking, exhaustion, recovery, and effects across generations. Organizational level covers roles in action, relationships or teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. Causal level covers events of undertaking, mechanisms of interaction between actions and costs, meso-level relational structures, institutional arrangements of responsibility, and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers original actions and costs, sequences of undertaking, cases involving actors, distributions of costs and voluntariness, consequence metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct actors and beneficiaries, dependents, those indirectly taking over, second-order spillovers, and impacts across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers dependencies in care, links of benefit, alternative arrangements for taking over, exit paths, and cost networks across domains. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for demands to take up work, resource use, refusal, stopping, protection, and remedy separately. Differences in voluntariness, costs, and exit need to be preserved.</p><p>Effective object: Human arrangements for undertaking in which action, cost, direction, voluntariness, conditions for refusal, and consequences need to be examined separately. Routes one, two, and three are judged under their own conditions, without first screening actions by their structural consequences.</p><p>Kept across scales: Costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, consequences, and rights to stop.</p><p>Must be added when moving to a larger scale: Distribution of voluntariness; relations of representation; cost spillovers; a genuine means of exit and protection provided through representatives.</p><p>Changes with scale: Forms of undertaking, positions bearing costs, and beneficiaries can change.</p><p>Objects to which it does not apply: Nonhuman systems without intentionality, voluntariness, responsibility, or the capability for meaning.</p><p>Cannot be raised to: Individual undertaking raised to a group duty; the concept of human undertaking transferred into the nonhuman core.</p><ol start="4"><li>State, Evidence, and Relations Between Variables</li></ol><p>States: Candidate; voluntary and bounded; risk of coercion; unilateral exhaustion; stopping or exit.</p><p>Observables: Time and resources invested, opportunities forgone, and bodily and psychological costs of the action; whether refusal, exit, stopping, and renegotiation are genuinely available; direction of the action, positions receiving benefits, and detectable structural consequences; alternative explanations such as coercion, fear, dependence, interests, and acting for show.</p><p>Evidence requirements: Actions and costs; conditions for refusal and exit; alternative explanations; benefits and consequences.</p><p>Inputs: Orientation anchor (§7.3.3); action uptake layer (§7.3.5); conditional potential field (§7.3.8); power and safety.</p><p>Outputs: Distribution of costs; states of relationships and institutions; stopping and repair.</p><p>Time window and lag: State immediate costs, sustained undertaking, exhaustion, and recovery lags.</p><p>Uncertainty: Record dependence, fear, hidden coercion, and safety of expression.</p><p>Local exclusion zone: Positions where people cannot refuse safely, cannot exit, or are masked by moral pressure.</p><p>Affected positions: Actors; beneficiaries; dependents; people taking over.</p><ol start="5"><li>Uptake, Responsibility, Ceilings, and Correction</li></ol><p>Uptake carriers: Specific actors; relational practices; arrangements for care or responsibility.</p><p>Responsible parties: Those making demands; parties granting authorization; parties responsible as beneficiaries; parties responsible for remedy.</p><p>Normative status: Subject to the normative premise “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted.” It cannot be commanded or conscripted.</p><p>Judgment ceiling: Apply the limits for routes one, two, and three separately. Route one describes only costs, direction, risks, and candidate actions. With corresponding evidence, route two describes the action’s voluntariness and conditions for refusal and exit. Route three judges structural consequences within a limited scope only if the corresponding channel-effect test and causal conditions hold. Judgments at any level must not diagnose personality, prove love or goodness, infer a duty to undertake, or authorize real-world action. Ethical naming is separately subject to the requirements on normative premises in the governance clause “Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate” (§16.14). Reaching route three does not satisfy that requirement.</p><p>Action ceiling: This variable produces only descriptions of voluntariness, actual costs, direction, alternative explanations, structural consequences, and needs for stopping and protection. It does not authorize protective measures, demands to take up work, requisition of resources, or adjudication of personality. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures.</p><p>Counterexamples: One-sided sacrifice that cannot be refused is praised as love or responsibility; a claim of undertaking lacks evidence of the stated action or cost. Actual failed attempts and cases with unknown outcomes are recorded separately. Their action descriptions must not be categorically withdrawn for lack of structural success.</p><p>Appeal: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), actors can refuse to be represented and explain coercion, costs, alternative explanations, and restrictions on exit through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original judgment about the undertaking or the original chain of demands.</p><p>Restoration: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline revoke the undertaking designation and related demands, and actually restore the state of refusal, exit, records, and resources. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified.</p><p>The “nine-axis scale” item is not a single-axis “micro–macro” label but the complete record of nine axes: aggregation level, which must state units, population, distribution, and aggregation rules; spatial extent, meaning physical space and digital boundaries; time span, meaning time windows, lags, and cycles; organizational level, meaning roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies; causal level, meaning events, interaction mechanisms, meso-level structures, institutions, and systemic conditions; observation resolution, meaning original events, sequences, individual cases, distributions, metrics, and summaries, with compression loss recorded; scope of influence, meaning the direct, indirect, second-order, cross-domain, and intergenerational range of those affected; network topology scope; and scope of jurisdiction and authorization. An expansion of aggregation level, spatial extent, organizational level, or scope of influence does not automatically expand the scope of authorization. Observing more cannot confer greater authority to act.</p><p>Recorded identities and the identity criterion belong to the identity-criterion item of the object’s definition. Relationships and channels of effect are written in the “Inputs” and “Uptake carriers” items, or fall under the conditions for causal claims. Membership and affected positions are written in the “Affected positions” and “Local exclusion zone” items and in the items on observation. None of them can stand in for a scale axis. Network topology scope among the nine scale axes (written N) shares a letter with the five normative premises (N1–N5), and the two must not be conflated. Below, “the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion” refers to the latter (§14.3).</p><p>All eleven variables share the same limit on action. The variables themselves produce only descriptions, evidence gaps, and needs for action. They do not authorize real-world adjustments. Their output ceilings are as follows.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Descriptions or Needs This Variable Can Produce</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structural Domain</td><td>Candidate structural domains, boundary disputes, and needs for more evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Boundaries and Interfaces</td><td>Boundary states, interface barriers, exclusion risks, and testing needs</td></tr><tr><td>Orientation Anchor</td><td>Candidate anchors, diverse expressions, comparison results, and needs for more evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Generative Node</td><td>Candidate classifications by generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, state transitions, and needs for more evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Action Uptake Layer</td><td>Uptake carriers, responsible parties, costs, capacity, rights to stop, gaps in uptake, and needs to reduce load, add resources, or reallocate them</td></tr><tr><td>Drive–Uptake Chain</td><td>Chain connectivity, time lags, losses, interruptions, costs, and uptake needs</td></tr><tr><td>Feedback Write-Back</td><td>Intake, changes in institutional elements, execution, duration, gaps in write-back, and needs for procedural repair</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional Potential Field</td><td>Candidate channels through which conditions could have effects, differences across positions, masking of evidence, and needs to reduce risk</td></tr><tr><td>Structural Load</td><td>Load, capacity, recovery, local overload, and needs to reduce load and add resources</td></tr><tr><td>Evolutionary Phase</td><td>Component applicability, prototype matches, and mixed states under the selected route, limited explanations of phase mechanisms or historical conditional increments, the independent path status of “Orderly Exit and Benign Demise,” uncertainty, and observation needs</td></tr><tr><td>Open-Ended Undertaking Action</td><td>Voluntariness, actual costs, direction, alternative explanations, structural consequences, and needs for stopping and protection</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>However obvious these needs may seem, any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures. The variables must not authorize testing, load reduction, adding resources, repair, protection, attribution of responsibility, exploratory action, advancement, exit arrangements, or other action on their own.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"A.11"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{v as __pageData,y as default};
